{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791388968,"nonce":"PATjO6R+Tqa/tA29l06AzsAhv1M=","title":"Cranelift: align the register spill area after explicit stack slots","message":"The pinned Cranelift ABI rounds its explicit/dynamic stack-slot region to the machine word (8 bytes on x64), then places register spills directly after it. Regalloc aligns 128-bit spill indexes relative to that region, but the region can start 8 bytes off a 16-byte boundary. Generated FFT native code consequently uses movdqu at rsp+0x88, 0x98, 0xa8 and 0xb8 in its hottest loop.\n\nGDB samples on the NUC show FFT_transform_internal rsp mod 4096 = 240 in the saved 76bc7ca9 build, but 3952 in the current 1f619130 build. In the latter, the 16-byte spill at rsp+0x88 starts at byte 4088 and straddles a page boundary. Both builds produce identical normalized native streams for all 16 FFT variants. The first-iteration slowdown persists with the old dominator algorithm selected inside the new executable, disappears with a changed call stack (extra output or early-tier-up diagnostic), and the additional samples are in generated native code. Alignment is a concrete hypothesis, not yet a measured fix.\n\nTest a backend-wide zero-sized 16-aligned final explicit slot to align the register spill area without moving existing slots. This uses the public CLIF stack layout contract and avoids a language-specific workaround or unportable local dependency override. Diagnostic ZYNTAX_DISABLE_SPILL_ALIGNMENT restores the old layout for same-executable controls. Validate native spill offsets, stack values across host calls, OSR transfer, frontend conformance, and short/long LLVM-off NUC benchmarks. Keep the unrelated isolated egraph patch out of these measurements.","files":null}]}